        "title": "mask_missing now crashes on list-like values that replace_list can still pass",
        "explanation": "The old function accepted list-like values_to_mask and matched any of their elements. The new version assumes a scalar: `isna(value)` on an array result makes `if isna(value):` raise 'truth value of an array is ambiguous'. Block.replace_list still calls `missing.mask_missing(values, s[0])` for non-string dtypes, and it keeps any pair where `_can_hold_element(x)` is true. For numpy numeric blocks that check accepts list-likes (it infers their dtype via np.asarray). So `pd.Series([1,2,3]).replace([[1, 2]], [9])` matched before and now raises ValueError. A one-element inner list instead goes through broadcasting. The new docstring says the caller has ensured the value isn't list-like, but replace_list doesn't enforce that.",
        "suggestion": "Make callers enforce the precondition: in Block.replace_list, skip pairs where `is_list_like(x)` (or raise a clear TypeError). Alternatively, raise a clear error inside mask_missing when `lib.is_list_like(value)`, so users don't get an 'ambiguous truth value' error.",
        "evidence": "    if isna(value):",
